### PostgreSQL migration

Assuming the logical-delete flag is `deleted`:

```sql
ALTER TABLE travel_destination
    ADD COLUMN country_code varchar(2);

ALTER TABLE travel_destination
    ADD CONSTRAINT travel_destination_country_code_ck
    CHECK (
        country_code IS NULL
        OR country_code ~ '^[A-Za-z]{2}$'
    );

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX travel_destination_active_country_name_uq
    ON travel_destination (country_code, name)
    WHERE deleted = false;
```

If logical deletion uses a timestamp, use `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL` instead.

This is a partial **unique index**, because PostgreSQL cannot create a partial unique constraint. It enforces uniqueness only for non-deleted rows. Existing rows are safe because the new column is nullable and has no default. Multiple rows with `country_code IS NULL` may share the same `name`, since PostgreSQL treats nulls as distinct; non-null `(country_code, name)` pairs must be unique among active destinations.

