### PostgreSQL migration

Assuming the existing logical-delete column is `deleted`:

```sql
ALTER TABLE travel_destination
    ADD COLUMN country_code varchar(2);

ALTER TABLE travel_destination
    ADD CONSTRAINT travel_destination_country_code_length_ck
    CHECK (country_code IS NULL OR char_length(country_code) = 2);

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX travel_destination_active_country_name_uidx
    ON travel_destination (country_code, name)
    WHERE deleted = false;
```

The index creation intentionally fails if active duplicate `(country_code, name)` rows already exist; resolve those duplicates before applying the migration. Existing rows remain valid because `country_code` is nullable.

PostgreSQL treats `NULL` values as distinct in a unique index, so multiple non-deleted destinations with the same `name` and `country_code IS NULL` are allowed. If `NULL` must count as one shared value, use PostgreSQL 15+:

```sql
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX travel_destination_active_country_name_uidx
    ON travel_destination (country_code, name) NULLS NOT DISTINCT
    WHERE deleted = false;
```

Use the migration runner’s transaction conventions; `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` is an alternative for large tables but must run outside a transaction.

